+# Script performance diagnostics
+
+The page loads `script-performance.js`, which uses the Long Animation Frames API
+to identify scripts contributing to frames longer than 50 ms. It aggregates
+script execution across the page lifecycle, including available buffered entries
+from before the monitor started. No performance data is sent to a server.
+
+Use the website normally, then run this in the developer console:
+
+```js
+scriptPerformance.report();
+// Focus on scripts with at least 100 ms accumulated execution in long frames:
+scriptPerformance.report({ minDuration: 100 });
+// Get unrounded data for your own analysis:
+scriptPerformance.snapshot();
+// Stop collecting and retrieve the final results:
+scriptPerformance.disconnect();
+```
+
+Reports also appear when the page becomes hidden after new long frames are
+observed. Rows are ranked by accumulated script execution time, with invocation
+counts, longest execution, forced style/layout time, and the function name and
+character offset of the longest execution when available. All durations are in
+milliseconds. Start with the highest totals and inspect the named source in
+DevTools to investigate the function.
+
+This measures execution within long frames, not download time or every script
+invocation. Frame blocking time is reported separately and cannot be assigned
+entirely to one script. Rendering or several scripts may contribute to a frame.
+Browser attribution is incomplete (for example, cross-origin frames, extensions,
+and short script executions may not be attributed), so an empty report does not
+prove the page is fast. The current static page has no other scripts to measure.
+
+The API has limited browser support. Check `scriptPerformance.supported`; browsers
+without it skip observation and report that attribution is unavailable. Use a
+supporting Chrome or Edge release to collect data. Reports are local to the
+current page load and reset on navigation.
+
+Run the automated diagnostics checks with `npm test`.
